  "vulnerability_information": "##Summary:\n\n#Hi team \n\nwhen user add ==Referrals== to his account use https://crm.na1.insightly.com/users/referrals he will get id to his linked account so when he try to disable the linked account in ==CONNECTED APPLICATION SETTINGS== in the end point https://crm.na1.insightly.com/users/usersettings ==request== contain the id will be send https://crm.na1.insightly.com/Users/GoogleDisable/2023554\nThe vulnerable endpoint allows an authenticated user to change the email address associated with their account and ==Remove it==(disableit) However, it does not sufficiently verify that requests were intentionally performed by the user (e.g. by checking the values of HTTP headers a).\n\n\n#Step's\n1-First  we need to account to confirm the issue account ==A== and ==B==\n2-Go to https://crm.na1.insightly.com/users/referrals and add Google Referrals to both account's use two account's\n3-Now the linked account will have two id not the same\n4-From account ==A== Go to https://crm.na1.insightly.com/users/usersettingstry to disable the linked account   and capitsher the request use ==Burp proxy==\n5- Generate  the POC use the Disable Request \n6-when open the link from account ==B== the bug will be exploite\n\n#Proof-of-Concept (PoC)\nThis is the code used for the demonstration video attached to this report\n```\n<html>\n  <!-- CSRF PoC - generated by Burp Suite Professional -->\n  <body>\n  <script>history.pushState('', '', '/')</script>\n    <form action=\"https://crm.na1.insightly.com/Users/GoogleDisable/2026462\">\n      <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"&#95;pjax\" value=\"&#35;main\" />\n      <input type=\"submit\" value=\"Submit request\" />\n    </form>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\nwhen send the request the id will auto been change so victim linked account will be disabled\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker can exploit this vulnerability in order to disable account linked or take over accounts by associating them with an address controlled by the attacker, then performing a password reset. In order for the attack to be successful, the target user account must be associated with an unconfirmed email address, and the target user must visit an attacker-controlled website while logged in.",
